course: dictionary
lesson: 5802
title: Madness in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Madness is a noun, a naming word for a state or a kind of behaviour. Its everyday meaning is very foolish or dangerous behaviour. «It would be madness to swim in that storm.» The storm is the clue: swimming in it would be very risky, so madness means a plan that is silly and dangerous.
explain: Madness can also mean wild excitement or confusion. Look at the words around it. «the madness of the January sales» describes a noisy, crowded rush of shoppers, not anyone who is ill. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then test it.
explain: Be careful with the old meaning. Long ago, madness was used for serious mental illness, but that use is now unkind. «Many old words for madness are jokes, and real mental illness is no joke, so a kind person does not use them about someone who is truly unwell.» Say «mental illness» instead.
explain: Another old use means great anger. You may find it in old books, so check the sentence, and use a dictionary if the context is still unclear.
fact: Madness is usually uncountable, so we say «the madness», not «madnesses».
fact: In everyday talk, «madness» means very foolish or dangerous behaviour.
fact: «Madness» can also mean wild excitement or confusion, as in «the madness of the January sales».
fact: The old meaning of serious mental illness is now unkind, so say «mental illness» instead.
fact: Great anger is an old use of madness that you may find in old books.
exercise: «It would be madness to swim in that storm.» What does madness mean here? | Great anger | Wild and noisy excitement | A calm and sensible plan | *Very foolish and dangerous
exercise: «the madness of the January sales» What does madness mean here? | Very old | Very foolish | *Wild, noisy confusion | Calm and quiet
exercise: Which is the kind way to talk about someone who is truly unwell? | *Saying mental illness | Calling them mad | Using an old word for madness | Making a joke about madness
test: Why should a kind person avoid old words for madness when talking about someone truly unwell? | Because they are long words | *Because real mental illness is no joke, and the words can hurt | Because they are only used in stories | Because madness means happy
test: Which meaning of madness fits «It's madness!» said about a plan to climb a steep cliff in the dark? | *Very foolish or dangerous | Great anger | Serious mental illness | Wild excitement
exam: Madness is a noun, which means it is a | joining word | doing word | *naming word | describing word
